    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1216">

                <pb facs="1164" n="1152" sameAs="frus:frus1943v04/pg1152"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 Defense (M) Turkey/313: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Turkey</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Steinhardt</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ankara</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1943-02-05T16:00:00+02:00">February 5, 1943—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received February 6—5:24 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>233. My 226, February 4, paragraph 2.<anchor n="47" xml:id="d1216-f1"/> The
                    Foreign Office has directed that the cars carrying 450 tons of chrome ore from
                    the Guleman Mines and destined for Germany which are still in Turkey be stopped
                    in transit and returned to the mines. The 1500–ton Turkish ship rumored to load
                    chrome at Fethiye for Germany has not yet arrived at Fethiye and there is no
                    present indication as to when vessel will arrive.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Steinhardt</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1216-f1" n="47" xml:id="d1216-f1-content">
                <p>Not printed; the paragraph read: “One month has passed since expiration of our
                    contract yet only movement of chrome intended for Germany that we know of is 450
                    tons from Guleman which has been thwarted by a protest to Foreign Office as
                    coming from our stocks and a 1500–ton Turk ship rumored to load at Fethiye ‘in
                    the next 10 days’”. (811.20 Defense (M) Turkey/307)</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
